1015
01:11:02,210 --> 01:11:04,510
你不仅仅是让 AI 给出一个问题的答案，而是让它生成一大篇关于它将如何解决该问题的文字，
然后利用这些内容来尝试解决问题，或者根据你的做法，可能是一篇长文。
or maybe an essay worth of text, depending how you're doing it.

1016
01:11:04,910 --> 01:11:08,710
所以你会产生一些关于……你知道的，他们称之为对问题进行推理的内容。
So you ever produce this text about like, you know, they call it reasoning about the problem.

1017
01:11:08,974 --> 01:11:11,810
我们可以整天争论这到底是不是真正的推理，但这只是该领域里的称呼。
We could argue all day about whether it's true reasoning, that's just what it's called in the field.

1018
01:11:12,330 --> 01:11:15,550
它们对问题进行这种推理，然后基于此给出答案。
They produce this reasoning about the problem and then produce the answer from there.

1019
01:11:16,078 --> 01:11:28,990
你让它们，训练它们去解决一亿个这类难题，不知为何，它们会采用一些能帮助它们在第一阶段
预测所有这些文本、并在第二阶段解决所有这些问题的倾向。
You have them, you train them to solve a hundred million of these hard problems, and somehow they sort of adopt whatever tendencies
help them predict all of that text in the first phase and solve all those problems in the second phase.

1020
01:11:29,150 --> 01:11:30,570
这就是所谓的大语言模型。
And this is called a large language model.

1021
01:11:30,750 --> 01:11:35,830
当我们开始涉及推理和问题解决时，我们可能就不该再称它们为大语言模型了。
We probably should have stopped calling them large language models when we started doing the the reasoning and the problem-solving.

1022
01:11:36,430 --> 01:11:47,210
作为一名外行，我想听到的解释是：它听起来像是个词语机器，然后你把它变成了一个问题机器。
我心想，既然它是个词语机器，那它的风险是什么？
One of the things I want to hear explanation as a muggle, like I am, is it sounds like it's like a word machine and then you know you
made it like a problem machine and I go okay so I can solve problems over here and it's a word machine, what's the risk of this?

1023
01:11:48,046 --> 01:11:50,670
好，那我们先从“词语机器”这一部分开始谈起。
Yeah so let's take the the word machine part first.

1024
01:11:51,790 --> 01:11:58,210
预测人类写出的词语，往往需要解决比那些写下这些词的人更难的问题。
Predicting words that humans wrote often requires solving a harder problem than the human who wrote them.

1025
01:11:59,598 --> 01:12:08,730
假设你给一只老鼠注射一种药物。比如，你会把这种药物的化学性质写下来，
把它注入到老鼠体内，然后看到那只老鼠死掉。
So suppose that you go and inject a drug in a rat and you're like, you know, it's like you write down the chemical nature of the drug,
you inject it into the rat, you see that the rat dies.

1026
01:12:09,166 --> 01:12:12,070
于是你就会记录：当我把那种药注入老鼠时，老鼠死了。
And so you're like, when I put that drug into the rat, the rat died.

1027
01:12:13,326 --> 01:12:20,830
现在，假设你在训练一个 AI，这个 AI 看到药物的化学
性质，它看到我把那药注入到老鼠时，老鼠……（空白）。
Now, suppose you're training an AI and the AI sees the chemical nature
of the drug, it sees when I put that drug into the rat, the rat blank.

1028
01:12:22,638 --> 01:12:25,970
记录下这些内容的人可以直观地看到老鼠发生了什么。
The human who wrote it down gets to just look at what happened to the rat.

1029
01:12:27,118 --> 01:12:30,690
而预测这些内容的 AI 则无法直接观察到老鼠的情况。
The AI predicting what was written does not get to just look at the rat.

1030
01:12:31,566 --> 01:12:37,770
所以，训练 AI 去预测人类文本，实际上是在训练它们变得可能比人类更聪明。
So training AIs to predict human text is training them to be potentially smarter than the humans.

1031
01:12:39,982 --> 01:12:48,390
因为它们必须能够回答这些问题，它们必须能够进行预测，
它们必须能够填补那些人类记录下所见内容时的空白处。
Because they need to be able to answer these questions, they need to be able to predict, They need to be able to, like,
fill in the blanks where humans were writing down what they saw.

1032
01:12:48,930 --> 01:12:52,510
梅森。只是为了让我从技术层面理解一下，它们并没有掌握任何知识，对吧？
Mason And there's, just so I understand technologically, there is no knowledge they have, though.

1033
01:12:52,622 --> 01:13:01,370
每一次，虽然有办法来缓解这些问题，但每一次它实际上都在
重新阅读，但由于训练的存在，它在某些方面变得更加准确。
Each time, and there are ways of kind of mitigating these, each time it is effectively
rereading, but because of training, it gets more accurate at certain things.

1034
01:13:01,950 --> 01:13:06,650
戴维。我的意思是，无论如何，当你调节参数时，它总会获取到其中的信息，
只是我们不知道它是怎么做到的。
David I mean, somehow, as you tune the knobs, somehow, it's getting information in there, and we don't know how.

1035
01:13:06,870 --> 01:13:08,270
弗拉基米尔。所以，情况其实比那要简单得多。
Vladimir So, it's much easier than that.

1036
01:13:08,730 --> 01:13:09,910
我们是人类，我们有大脑。
We're humans, we have a brain.

1037
01:13:10,210 --> 01:13:11,550
大脑是由神经元组成的。
Brains are made of neurons.

1038
01:13:11,550 --> 01:13:16,610
然后我们试图在电脑上复制它，我们简化了它，但我们创建了一个神经网络。
Then we try to copy that on a computer, we simplify it, but we create a neural network.

1039
01:13:16,974 --> 01:13:18,470
所以我们正在制造人造大脑。
So we're making artificial brains.

1040
01:13:18,690 --> 01:13:26,550
就像人类大脑一样，在认知科学领域，我们并不真正了解你如何运作、如何学习，
或者你的大脑中哪些记忆被存储在哪里。
Just like with human brains, with cognitive science, we don't really understand how you function, how you learn,
where in your brain certain memories are stored.

1041
01:13:26,710 --> 01:13:31,870
我们只是初步了解到一些情况，比如当你看到一张脸时，这个神经元会放电。
We have some glimpses of understanding this neuron fires when you see a face.

1042
01:13:32,250 --> 01:13:33,650
但并没有完整的全貌。
But there is no complete picture.

1043
01:13:34,030 --> 01:13:40,870
因此很多时候你可以直观地理解正在发生的事情，然后你只需把它们当作“人工个体”来思考。
And so a lot of times you can get intuitive understanding of what's going on, then you just think about it as artificial persons.

1044
01:13:40,870 --> 01:13:43,550
这并不是精确的映射，但它有帮助。
It's not exact mapping, but it helps.

1045
01:13:43,630 --> 01:13:51,970
如果你让一个孩子接受 12 年的教育，他们会接触到大量的题目，然后他们毕业时，
在解决问题方面会变得更出色一点。
So if you send a child through 12 years of education, they get lots of problems to look at,
and then they graduate and become a little better at solving problems.

1046
01:13:52,290 --> 01:13:54,230
这就是我们在这里试图复制的东西。
This is what we're trying to replicate here.

1047
01:13:54,798 --> 01:14:00,510
人们抱怨训练那些非常，你知道的，高强度过程需要大量资金。
People complain that it takes a lot of money to train those very, you know, intense process.

1048
01:14:00,730 --> 01:14:02,910
但你忘了培养一个人类需要 20 年时间。
You forget that it takes 20 years to train a human.

1049
01:14:04,046 --> 01:14:09,130
而且它们并不是通用超智能，它们非常局限，如果能拿到学士学位我们就已经很幸运了。
And they are not general superintelligences, they are very narrow, we're lucky if they graduate with a bachelor's.

1050
01:14:09,390 --> 01:14:12,910
所以，很多方面其实是一模一样的。
So, a lot of it is exactly the same.

1051
01:14:13,010 --> 01:14:14,630
比如，我们能制造出安全的人类吗？
Can we make safe humans, for example?

1052
01:14:15,278 --> 01:14:21,230
我们发明了宗教、伦理和测谎测试，然而人类的安全问题依然没能解决。
We invented religion, ethics, lie detector tests, and yet human safety is still unsolved problem.

1053
01:14:21,590 --> 01:14:27,370
现在你面对的是更陌生的东西，它没有实体，也没有生理需求，所以会有额外的复杂性。
Now you have something more alien, doesn't have physical body, doesn't have biological needs, so there are additional complications.

1054
01:14:27,886 --> 01:14:31,730
但我们在人类身上面临的所有问题依然存在。
But all the problems we face with humans, still there.

1055
01:14:32,010 --> 01:14:34,310
安全问题、犯罪，这些都会存在。
Safety problems, crime, all that stays.

1056
01:14:34,670 --> 01:14:36,130
还有理解上的问题。
And problems with understanding.

1057
01:14:36,250 --> 01:14:38,010
是什么驱动人类去做某件事？
What motivates a human to do something?

1058
01:14:38,510 --> 01:14:40,310
为什么我们会患上心理疾病？
Why do we get mental disorders?

1059
01:14:40,654 --> 01:14:41,870
这些问题都会出现在这里。
All that shows up there.

1060
01:14:42,630 --> 01:14:48,910
而且我们至今仍无法搞清楚，如果一个连环杀手被检查大脑，
我们往往无法准确判断他为什么要决定去杀死一群人。
And we still don't, if someone is a serial killer and we look at their brain, we
can't often figure out exactly why they made the decision to kill a bunch of people.

1061
01:14:48,910 --> 01:14:52,430
你也不能说，噢，我去改变这些神经元，让他们不再成为连环杀手。
And you can't be like, oh, I'll go change these neurons so that they stop being a serial killer.

1062
01:14:52,590 --> 01:14:55,070
我们对 AI 也没有这种能力。
We just like don't have that capacity with the AIs.

1063
01:14:55,170 --> 01:15:01,650
这是一个核心问题，人们想知道的是，在面对 AI 时是否存在一种控制的幻觉。
This is one of the big questions that people want to know is there's this sort of illusion of control with AI.

1064
01:15:02,410 --> 01:15:10,830
如果我们甚至不完全了解现代神经网络是如何思考的，那公司凭什么相信他们能控制任何形式的
超智能？如果连它们怎么思考我们都不懂的话。
If we don't even fully understand how modern neural networks think, Why do companies believe they can control any form of
superintelligence if we don't understand how they think?

1065
01:15:11,750 --> 01:15:17,230
如果他们理解了系统如何运作，情况反而更糟，因为递归自我改进会变得容易得多。
It's worse if they understood how the system works, then recursive self-improvement becomes much easier.

1066
01:15:17,646 --> 01:15:19,070
你会迎来更快的爆发。
You get faster takeoff.

1067
01:15:19,190 --> 01:15:22,370
目前，模型并不理解它自己的思考过程。
Right now, the model doesn't understand its own thinking.

1068
01:15:23,182 --> 01:15:25,770
安迪，我们了解这些系统是如何思考的吗？
Do we understand how these systems think, Andy?

1069
01:15:26,010 --> 01:15:27,110
我是说，我同意。
I mean, I agree.

1070
01:15:27,210 --> 01:15:29,370
在一些非常重要的方面上，它们都是黑盒。
These are black boxes in some pretty important ways.

1071
01:15:29,530 --> 01:15:32,450
只是我没那么害怕，不像其他人那样害怕。
I'm just less terrified by that than a lot of other people are.

1072
01:15:32,530 --> 01:15:34,510
有很多事情我们并不了解得很透彻。
There are lots of things we don't understand very well.

1073
01:15:34,510 --> 01:15:37,750
我们能遏制那些我们无法完美理解的东西吗？
can we contain things that we don't understand perfectly?

1074
01:15:38,130 --> 01:15:38,690
是的，我们可以。
Yes, we can.

1075
01:15:38,790 --> 01:15:52,270
我认为 OpenAI 做得很糟糕，我们今天讨论过，他们为试图去利用、
去破解可能流向外部世界的安全问题而建立的 AI 遏制措施做得非常差。
I think OpenAI did, we've talked about it today, lousy job of building the containment for the AI that they stood up to try to
exploit, to try to crack security problems that went out into the outside world.

1076
01:15:52,370 --> 01:16:00,930
他们在构建虚拟沙箱方面做得很烂，它本该是模型必须待在里面的地方，但它没能留在那儿。
They did a lousy job of building the virtual sandbox that it was where it was
supposed to have to, where it was supposed to remain, and it didn't remain.

1077
01:16:01,310 --> 01:16:02,870
这并不意味着不可能。
That doesn't mean that it's impossible.

1078
01:16:02,870 --> 01:16:05,370
这意味着 open AI 做得相当糟糕。
It means open AI did a pretty bad job.

1079
01:16:05,530 --> 01:16:08,350
SIMON 那这是人类和他们的智能造成的后果吗？
SIMON And is that a function of those humans and their intelligence?

1080
01:16:08,846 --> 01:16:11,230
DAVID 我认为这只是因为安全协议太烂了。
DAVID I think it's just a function of a pretty lousy security protocol.

1081
01:16:11,630 --> 01:16:12,930
SIMON 是基于人类的智能。
SIMON Based from human intelligence.

1082
01:16:13,390 --> 01:16:19,370
既然沙箱是靠人类智能构建的，那听起来可能就是人类智能出现了缺陷。
The idea that sandbox was built by human intelligence, it sounds like there was a deficit in human intelligence, potentially.

1083
01:16:20,270 --> 01:16:23,910
DAVID 当然，但毕竟总有人会把车开到电线杆上。
DAVID Sure, but there are, you know, people who drive cars in the telephone pole.

1084
01:16:24,010 --> 01:16:25,510
那是不是意味着我们不会开车？
Does that mean we can't drive?

1085
01:16:25,810 --> 01:16:25,930
SIMON 不会。
SIMON No.

1086
01:16:25,950 --> 01:16:26,990
DAVID 我们不应该让它们变得超级智能。
DAVID We shouldn't make them super intelligent.

1087
01:16:27,150 --> 01:16:29,890
SIMON 不，但你不能说……我是说，从某种程度上讲。
SIMON No, but you wouldn't, I mean, arguably.

1088
01:16:30,770 --> 01:16:32,530
这就是我们目前试图解决的问题。
Like this is what we're trying to solve for at the moment.

1089
01:16:32,530 --> 01:16:35,850
不，事实是，我不知道细节。
No, the fact is, I don't know the details.

1090
01:16:36,010 --> 01:16:42,010
我觉得他们在建立这个受限环境时犯了一些相当基础的错误。
It feels to me like they made some fairly basic mistakes in setting up this confined environment.

1091
01:16:42,230 --> 01:16:43,850
我认为在 OpenAI 的案例中并非如此。
I think that wasn't true in the OpenAI case.

1092
01:16:43,970 --> 01:16:45,530
在很多情况下都是真的，但在 OpenAI 的案例里不是。
It was true in a lot of the cases, but not the OpenAI one.

1093
01:16:45,530 --> 01:16:49,690
这并不意味着我们无法控制这个黑盒。
That doesn't mean that we are unable to control this black box.

1094
01:16:49,750 --> 01:16:51,110
这并不一定能推导出来。
That does not necessarily follow.

1095
01:16:51,110 --> 01:16:51,590
我明白你的意思。
I get that.

1096
01:16:51,726 --> 01:17:02,660
当人类试图去限制一个比自己更聪明的东西时，逻辑上可以推断出，如果那个东西比我聪明，
它就更擅长发现我系统中的漏洞或弱点……
It's just at a time when you've got a human trying to contain something that is smarter than it,
one would logically conclude that if the thing is smarter than I am and I'm trying to contain it,

1097
01:17:02,660 --> 01:17:06,990
当人类试图去限制一个比自己更聪明的东西时，逻辑上可以推断出，如果那个东西比我聪明，
它就更擅长发现我系统中的漏洞或弱点……
it would be better at knowing the exploits or vulnerabilities in my own...

1098
01:17:07,450 --> 01:17:10,450
这就好比说，如果你把爱因斯坦关进监狱，你就永远无法控制他。
That's like saying, if you put Einstein in a jail, you could never contain him.

1099
01:17:10,510 --> 01:17:11,510
我不认同这个观点。
I don't agree with that.

1100
01:17:11,950 --> 01:17:15,650
如果让他连上互联网，而且他拥有数字思维，那就是个不同的问题了。
Put him in jail with an internet connection, and he has a digital mind, that's a different question.

1101
01:17:15,650 --> 01:17:17,830
是的，这可能是一个更贴切的类比。
Yeah, that's probably a more apt analogy.

1102
01:17:18,250 --> 01:17:20,390
派松鼠去把爱因斯坦关起来。
Get squirrels, keep Einstein imprisoned.

1103
01:17:20,890 --> 01:17:21,670
这就是问题所在。
That's the question.

1104
01:17:22,210 --> 01:17:28,930
至于那次黑客攻击，据我所知，他们发现了 zero-day 利用程序，
这意味着完全是人类从未见过的全新漏洞。
The hacking accident, as far as I know, they found zero-day exploits, which means completely novel exploits no human knew about.

1105
01:17:29,070 --> 01:17:32,570
这不仅仅是设置不当，密码的质量也是个问题。
It wasn't just poor setup, the password is, you know, quality.

1106
01:17:33,050 --> 01:17:35,110
这是一次全新的逃逸。
It was a brand new escape.

1107
01:17:35,170 --> 01:17:35,970
这是多个 zero-day 漏洞。
It was multiple zero days.

1108
01:17:36,206 --> 01:17:39,690
所以 zero-day 攻击是指防御者还没有时间去处理的攻击。
So a zero-day attack is an attack that the defenders have had zero days to handle.

1109
01:17:40,090 --> 01:17:41,690
这是网络安全的术语。
It's cybersecurity lingo.

1110
01:17:43,246 --> 01:17:49,050
所以当我们说它们使用 zero-day 攻击时，我们的意
思是这些 AI 在软件中发现了人类根本不知道的漏洞。
And so when we say that they use zero-day attacks, what we mean is that these
AIs were finding bugs in the software that the humans had no knowledge of.

1111
01:17:49,550 --> 01:17:51,230
而且它们发现了很多这样的漏洞。
And they were finding multiple of these bugs.

1112
01:17:51,662 --> 01:17:53,250
通常情况下，其中一个漏洞不足以让你逃脱出来。
One of these bugs usually doesn't let you break out.

1113
01:17:53,350 --> 01:17:59,150
这有点像如果你在这里发现了一道墙缝，又在另一边的墙外发现了一道裂缝，
那你只需要挖一点点就能把这两处裂缝连通。
It's sort of like if you find a crack in the wall over here and you find a crack on the outside of the wall over there,
then you just need to dig a little bit to connect those cracks.

1114
01:18:00,210 --> 01:18:03,910
如果你只找到一个漏洞，你就没法在黑市上把它卖到几百万美元。
You can't sell those for millions of dollars on the dark market if you find one.

1115
01:18:03,970 --> 01:18:04,910
所以这很难
So it's difficult

1116
01:18:04,910 --> 01:18:05,750
去寻找。
to find.

1117
01:18:06,030 --> 01:18:15,610
为了让听众也能理解，我想确认一下，zero-day 总是指从未被用过来破解任何东西的新方法，
还是仅仅针对特定情况的特殊手段？
In how, just so I understand for the listeners as well, is a zero day always a novel way that no one has ever used to break anything
before or is it just for the unique situation?

1118
01:18:16,050 --> 01:18:21,730
比如它是 hugging face 上的某个东西的 zero-day，还是说是一种全新的通用黑客攻击手段？
Like so was it a zero day for a thing in a hugging phase versus a novel new way of hacking in general?

1119
01:18:21,934 --> 01:18:26,290
嗯，所以它们并不是什么完全全新的黑客技术之类的。
Um, so it was, uh, they weren't, like, totally novel hacking techniques or anything.

1120
01:18:26,370 --> 01:18:27,610
这就是我当时在想的……
That's kind of why I was getting...

1121
01:18:27,610 --> 01:18:32,670
不是说它不严重，而是说，它发明了一种全新的做事方法，这两者之间是有区别的。
Not to say it's not bad, but just, like, there's a difference between it came up with a brand new way to do something.

