I think you may be right that like we hit a wall.

1925
02:11:09,340 --> 02:11:14,420
你可能是对的，或许缺少了一些基础的东西，比如他
们现在的那一步，也就是 2027 年，可能跨度太大了。
You may be right that there's some fundamental thing missing like that one of their steps now, 2027, just like steps too far.

1926
02:11:14,420 --> 02:11:21,900
我希望并祈祷那是真的，但考虑到我们所看到的种种，
我不认为我们可以排除 2027 年发生这种情况的可能性。
I hope and pray that's true, but I don't think we can rule out this happening in 2027, given what we have seen.

1927
02:11:22,638 --> 02:11:36,068
我认为我们不能排除这种可能：如果你拿我们现有的东西，推演三个月，
然后投入一万个智能体集群去构建更好的 AI 架构，并且成功了。
I think we cannot rule out that you take the stuff that we have, you project it forward three months,
and you put an agent swarm 10,000 strong on making a better AI architecture, and it succeeds.

1928
02:11:37,326 --> 02:11:42,180
据我所知，递归自我改进可能在 12 月就开始。
For all I know, recursive self-improvement could begin in December.

1929
02:11:42,180 --> 02:11:46,560
它不需要变得好得多，只要稍微好一点点，并且在不断变好。
It doesn't have to be a lot better, it just has to be a little bit better and getting better.

1930
02:11:46,900 --> 02:11:48,240
一旦你开启了这个循环。
Once you start the cycle.

1931
02:11:48,760 --> 02:11:51,020
我不会押注于此，事实上我会反向押注。
I wouldn't bet on this, I would in fact bet against it.

1932
02:11:51,630 --> 02:12:05,840
但考虑到我们所看到的、这些家伙对预测的精准把握、正在推出的技术、
还有那些智能体集群和千禧年问题，我认为在六个月内将概率设为低于 1% 是很难做到的。
But like given what we've seen, given these guys nailing the predictions, given what's coming out,
like given the swarms and given the millennium problems, I think it's kind of hard to have less than 1% in six months.

1933
02:12:06,180 --> 02:12:12,140
原因之一是，你知道，当所有这些前沿实验室的 CEO，
比如 Dario 和 Sam，他们开始谈论这些事情时。
One of the reasons why, you know when all these Frontier Lab CEOs like Dario and Sam and they will start talking about this stuff.

1934
02:12:12,718 --> 02:12:20,440
就激励结构而言，我认为如果他们的团队成员知道，
而他们却不公开讨论这些，那么他们的团队就会离职。
In terms of incentive structure, I think that if their teams know, and
they're not out publicly talking about it, then their teams will quit.

1935
02:12:21,070 --> 02:12:31,440
所以，我认为科技领域出现这种我们从未见过的奇怪文化——团队成员在发推特，
CEO 也在推特上谈论危险——是因为正如我们在开头提到的那个人，Jacob？
So one of the reasons why I think you have this strange culture in tech we've never seen before where team members are tweeting,
and the CEO is tweeting about the dangers, is because as the guy we mentioned at the start, Jacob?

1936
02:12:31,880 --> 02:12:32,380
Coxon，是的。
Coxon, yeah.

1937
02:12:32,480 --> 02:12:34,320
他谈到了他们在 Slack 频道里在聊什么。
He talks about what's going on in their Slack channels.

1938
02:12:34,926 --> 02:12:39,020
他提到，在他们的 Slack 频道里，他们正在讨论潜在的灾难。
He talks about, in their Slack channels, they're talking about the potential catastrophe.

1939
02:12:39,020 --> 02:12:46,740
所以，我认为 Dario 为了留住他的团队成员，必须公开表态：顺便说一下，
我们正接近递归自我改进，这就是他一直在做的事情。
So, I think that Dario, in order to retain his team members, needs to be out front saying, by the way,
we're getting closer to recursive self-improvement, which is what he's been doing.

1940
02:12:46,900 --> 02:12:50,900
而且我认为 Sam 也必须公开说明那些巨大的风险。
And I think Sam has to also publicly say the big dangers.

1941
02:12:51,100 --> 02:12:53,240
所以，人们经常说，噢，他们是因为这个原因和那个原因才这么说的。
So, people often say, oh, they're saying that for this reason and that.

1942
02:12:53,486 --> 02:12:55,860
我认为如果他们不公开表态，就留不住员工。
I think if they don't say that publicly, they don't retain their employees.

1943
02:12:56,240 --> 02:12:58,080
例如，在我的公司里，我们有 200 人。
For example, in my company, we have 200 people.

1944
02:12:58,510 --> 02:13:08,100
如果内部我们在讨论真正的风险，而且这个风险威胁到人
类，而我在接受采访时却不提这件事，那我就麻烦大了。
If internally, we were discussing a real risk, and I, that had a threat to humanity, and then when I was doing interviews,
I wasn't mentioning it, I would be in big trouble.

1945
02:13:08,974 --> 02:13:10,780
因为我的团队成员也会去参加采访。
Because my team members would go do interviews as well.

1946
02:13:10,880 --> 02:13:12,760
他们会辞职并说，顺便提一下，史蒂芬已经知情了。
They would quit and say, by the way, Stephen is aware.

1947
02:13:13,080 --> 02:13:15,460
就像我们这些，我甚至不敢这么说，一些社交网络的情况一样。
Kind of what we sort of, dare I say, some of these social networks.

1948
02:13:15,800 --> 02:13:16,380
我完全同意。
I totally agree.

1949
02:13:16,558 --> 02:13:18,020
那些在团队成员离职的社交网络上的举报者。
The whistleblowers at these social networks where team members left.

1950
02:13:18,020 --> 02:13:21,140
这比说“这有助于卖掉公司”更有道理。
It makes more sense than saying that this helps to sell the company.

1951
02:13:21,280 --> 02:13:23,220
我的产品会杀死所有人，快来买。
My product will kill everyone, buy it.

1952
02:13:23,260 --> 02:13:25,300
而且作为一个责任问题，我觉得这已经失控了。
And as a liability issue, there's a- I think it's out of control though.

1953
02:13:25,480 --> 02:13:31,200
我认为他们最初可能……我认为公司内部确实有人对安全问题感到非常担忧。
I think that they may have at first, I think that there are people within the companies who have very real worries about safety.

1954
02:13:31,280 --> 02:13:33,160
我不认为他们全都是愤世嫉俗的。
I don't think it's all of them are cynical.

1955
02:13:33,160 --> 02:13:39,880
然而，我认为“庞大且可怕”的叙事其实是一种失控的营销手段。
I do, however, think the it's so big and scary narrative was a marketing tactic that got out of control.

1956
02:13:40,078 --> 02:13:46,180
现在出现了真正的伤害，因为问题就在于，如果他
们早些时候真心在乎安全，他们本可以做得更好。
And now there are actual real harms, because here's the thing, if they were sincere about safety earlier,
they would have done a much better job with it.

1957
02:13:46,380 --> 02:13:48,580
在这些家伙成立公司之前，我就认识很多他们。
I knew a lot of these guys before they started their companies.

1958
02:13:49,294 --> 02:13:51,060
我认为这里有一些情况需要解释。
I think there is something to explain here.

1959
02:13:51,566 --> 02:13:58,340
我觉得这些人居然说“我们正在构建的技术可能有巨大的风险杀掉所有人”，这有点疯狂。
I think it's like kind of crazy that these guys are like, we are
building technology that we think has a big risk of killing everybody.

1960
02:13:58,766 --> 02:14:00,040
我们正亲手去制造它。
We're building it with our bare hands.

1961
02:14:00,040 --> 02:14:00,740
嗯……
Um,

1962
02:14:00,740 --> 02:14:04,860
而且我认为你必须问问，为什么这些人会这么说？
and I think you got to ask why, why would people be saying that?

1963
02:14:06,600 --> 02:14:15,640
我认为部分原因正如你所说的，他们实际上需要留
住那些看到“集群”逃脱、尽管他们试图阻止的员工。
And I think part of it is what you said, that they actually sort of need to retain the employees who are seeing the swarms escape
despite their attempts to make them not escape.

1964
02:14:15,982 --> 02:14:21,860
如果公司高层不承认许多员工所相信的这些可能性，很多员工就会辞职并抗议。
And a lot of them will like quit and protest if the guys at the top of the company
aren't acknowledging the possibilities here that a lot of the employees believe in.

1965
02:14:22,360 --> 02:14:31,100
我认为你看到的很多情况是那些担心这个问题的人，但他们依然选择去创办公司。
I think a lot of what you're seeing here is guys that are worried about it,
but they're sort of guy who worries about it that starts the company anyway.

1966
02:14:33,280 --> 02:14:42,900
回到2015年，当我们讨论这些问题时，我正和这些人交流，Miri 是在2000年成立的。
Back in 2015 when we were having these conversations where like I was having
some of these conversations with these guys, Miri was started in the year 2000.

1967
02:14:43,520 --> 02:14:46,320
早在这些家伙出现之前，我们就在研究人工智能的发展方向。
We have been looking at where AI is going since before any of these guys.

1968
02:14:46,600 --> 02:14:51,280
他们来咨询这些问题时找的就是我们，而他们必须找到一种方法去淡化这些问题以继续推进。
We were the guys that they talked to about this stuff and that they had to find a way to dismiss to go ahead.

1969
02:14:51,280 --> 02:14:52,140
对吧？
right?

1970
02:14:52,814 --> 02:15:01,320
2015年那些被人工智能威力吸引的人，同样也被其危险性所警醒。
Most people who could be sold on the power of AI in 2015 were also sold on the dangers of AI in 2015.

1971
02:15:02,190 --> 02:15:09,320
那些创办公司的人是那些能够说服自己“我必须去做这件事”的人。
The sort of guys who start the companies are the ones who are able to convince themselves, I need to be the one to do it.

1972
02:15:10,860 --> 02:15:12,140
这就是动机的核心吗？
Is that the crux of the motivation?

1973
02:15:12,878 --> 02:15:21,220
因为我曾作为旁听者参与过与一些前沿实验室领导者的私人对话，
他们都是非常资深且有影响力的朋友。
Because I've had, I've been second party to private conversations with some of the leaders of the Frontier Labs from good,
good friends of minds that are very connected.

1974
02:15:21,700 --> 02:15:26,560
其中一位前沿实验室的首席执行官私下里跟我提到了这一点。
and they told me that one particular Frontier Lab CEO estimates privately to him.

1975
02:15:26,600 --> 02:15:32,020
顺便说一下，当我邀请他参加播客时，我甚至看过他们交流时的文字信息。
and by the way, I've seen literal text messages of them in conversation when I asked him to come on the podcast.

1976
02:15:32,160 --> 02:15:32,680
所以他当时就说，『噢。
and so he was like, "Oh.

1977
02:15:32,680 --> 02:15:32,800
对。
yeah.

1978
02:15:32,846 --> 02:15:33,540
我给他发过短信。』你看。
I've texted him .". Look.

1979
02:15:34,894 --> 02:15:36,600
他说了不，顺便说一下，我觉得这挺好笑的。
He said no, by the way, which I found kind of funny.

1980
02:15:37,710 --> 02:15:43,480
他说，这位特定的 AI 公司 CEO 认为人类灭绝的概率大约在 10% 左右。
Where he said to me, this particular AI CEO thinks that the probability is roughly around 10% of human extinction.

1981
02:15:43,854 --> 02:15:45,180
我觉得他说是 8%。
I think he said 8%.

1982
02:15:45,422 --> 02:15:50,840
当我听到这个时，我之所以经常讨论这个问题，是因为我在采访中看到他说的内容并不一致。
And when I heard that, part of the reason I have so many conversations
about this is because I see him in interviews saying other things.

1983
02:15:51,820 --> 02:15:52,580
而且我信任我的朋友。
And I trust my friend.

1984
02:15:53,420 --> 02:16:03,580
所以我开始思考，这就是为什么我用桌上这些按钮的思维实验，
因为那位特定的 AI 公司 CEO 认为 100 个按钮中有 8 个会导致灭绝，但他们还是在通电。
So I then wonder, this is why I use the thought experiment of these buttons on the table,
because that particular AICO thinks that eight of the hundred buttons are going to cause extinction and they're powering on anyway.

1985
02:16:04,110 --> 02:16:05,940
人类这样做的动机是什么？
What is the human motivation to do that?

1986
02:16:06,000 --> 02:16:06,560
我问了我的朋友。
I asked my friend.

1987
02:16:06,620 --> 02:16:11,860
我的朋友说，嗯，你知道，这是他说的，再次强调，这是间接信息，所以可能不是真的。
My friend said, well, you know, this is what he said, and again, it's second-party information so it might not be true.

1988
02:16:12,014 --> 02:16:12,820
这有点像传话游戏。
It's a bit of a Chinese whispers.

1989
02:16:12,820 --> 02:16:24,490
他说，这个人即便会导致人类灭绝，也想成为那个做出那件事的人。
is, he said, this particular person, even if it caused human extinction,
would like to have the significance of the person that did that thing.

1990
02:16:24,810 --> 02:16:26,290
因为那将意味着……
Because that would be a…

1991
02:16:26,290 --> 02:16:28,790
我觉得你在道德上就该告诉我们到底是谁。
I think you're ethically required to tell us who the fuck it is.

1992
02:16:29,090 --> 02:16:30,550
是其中一家前沿实验室的 CEO，而且不是 Dario。
It's one of the Frontier Labs CEOs and it's not Daria.

1993
02:16:31,790 --> 02:16:33,250
但 Dario 曾是 CEO。
But Daria was the CEO.

1994
02:16:33,430 --> 02:16:35,830
但我不知道，这些都是传话过来的，所以我也不确定。
But I don't know, these things are Chinese whispers, so I don't know.

1995
02:16:36,150 --> 02:16:38,950
我觉得你其实可以获取第一手信息。
I think that you can actually get this info firsthand.

1996
02:16:39,930 --> 02:16:41,430
马斯克在这方面态度很明确。
Elon Musk is clear about this.

1997
02:16:41,806 --> 02:16:47,968
他，他有……他在去年的一个采访中说，我最初不想涉足这些 AI 领域，因为我觉得这太危险了。
He, he has, uh, he did an interview last year where he was like, I didn't
want to get into this AI stuff because I thought I was too dangerous.

1998
02:16:48,590 --> 02:16:50,670
但我后来意识到，无论有没有我，这都会发生。
But then I realized it was going to happen with or without me.

1999
02:16:51,022 --> 02:16:53,629
于是我决定，与其做旁观者，我宁愿成为参与者。
And I decided to, I'd rather be a participant than a spectator.

2000
02:16:54,170 --> 02:16:56,709
因为谷歌表示他们要追求这个目标，而他不信任谷歌。
Because Google said that they were going to pursue it and he didn't trust Google.

2001
02:16:57,134 --> 02:16:57,530
没错。
That's right.

2002
02:16:57,790 --> 02:17:03,629
你知道，你可以在流出的——抱歉，不是流出的，是在诉讼过程中公开的 OpenAI 邮件中看到这些。
You know, you can see in the leaked, or sorry, not leaked the, the open AI emails that came out during the discovery and court cases.

2003
02:17:03,950 --> 02:17:13,730
你可以看到这些人在讨论：我们必须确保是我们和我们在
OpenAI 的非营利组织来控制这个，而不是让谷歌的人去控制。
You can see these guys discussing in the threads, like we need to make sure that we and
our non-profit at OpenAI control this instead of the people at Google controlling this.

2004
02:17:14,230 --> 02:17:22,670
然后当然，OpenAI 最初作为非营利组织成立，后来变成了一种营利性机构，
关于有多少非营利资金在某种程度上被挪用的讨论很多。
And then of course, OpenAI was founded as a non-profit and then it was sort of changed into a for-profit and there's much debate about
how much of that non-profit money was in some sense stolen.

2005
02:17:23,330 --> 02:17:26,870
所以马斯克也离开了，因为他认为他们不会成为好的管理者。
And so Elon also left because he thought they weren't going to be good stewards.

2006
02:17:27,030 --> 02:17:29,730
Dario 也离开去创立 Anthropic，是因为……
Dario also left to create Anthropic because ...

2007
02:17:29,730 --> 02:17:41,870
所以从某种意义上说，除了由 Demis Hassabis 最初的初创公司演变出来的 Google 那家，
其他的 AI 实验室之所以存在，是因为这些 CEO 都不信任其他人。
So in some sense, all of these AI labs except the Google one that came out of Demis Hassabis' original startup,
all of the other AI labs exist because none of the CEOs trust the other guys.

2008
02:17:42,798 --> 02:17:46,690
这些 CEO 都不认为让别人来掌控超智能的缰绳是合适的。
None of the CEOs think the other guy should be the one holding the leash on the superintelligence.

2009
02:17:46,888 --> 02:17:47,910
他们谁也不信任谁。
None of them trust each other.

2010
02:17:48,846 --> 02:17:50,150
我只是少信任了一个人。
I just trust one fewer.

2011
02:17:51,530 --> 02:17:52,010
是的。
Yeah.

2012
02:17:53,490 --> 02:17:55,010
Andy，你的总结陈词是什么？
What are your closing thoughts, Andy?

2013
02:17:56,709 --> 02:17:58,570
我们正处在一个非常有趣的时代。
We're living in really interesting times.

2014
02:17:59,450 --> 02:18:12,490
我认为你们提出了一个非常好的论点，即这些系统正在展示出全新的能力，
而且这些能力非常强大，需要我们做出回应。
And I think you guys have made a very good argument that these systems are demonstrating new capabilities,
which are very powerful and which demand a response.

2015
02:18:13,358 --> 02:18:17,790
比起你，我更相信我们有能力应对这一挑战。
I'm much more confident in our ability to rise to that challenge than you are.

2016
02:18:18,734 --> 02:18:20,530
但你承认存在这种风险。
But you accept the existential risk.

2017
02:18:22,308 --> 02:18:23,910
让我试着再解释一遍。
Let me try to say it again.

2018
02:18:24,941 --> 02:18:37,284
我意识到，随着这种如此执著、坚毅、具有智能体化特征，而且你知道的，
带点欺骗性的技术出现，必然会带来一些我们以前没见过的危害，我觉得用这个词很准确。
I appreciate that there are new harms we haven't seen before that come along with a technology that's this dogged, tenacious, agentic,
you know, deceptive, I think that's the right word for it.

2019
02:18:37,290 --> 02:18:37,968
我同意这一点。
I agree with that.

2020
02:18:38,150 --> 02:18:47,030
对于我们是否有能力有效应对世界上出现的这些新挑战，我比我的两位同事要乐观得多。
I am much more optimistic about our ability to respond effectively to that
new challenge out there in the world than I think my two colleagues are.

2021
02:18:47,410 --> 02:18:48,530
那你还会坚持认为是 0% 吗？
And would you still be at 0%?

2022
02:18:48,709 --> 02:18:51,010
在这次会议期间，我的初步判断没有改变。
My prior has not shifted during this meeting.

2023
02:18:51,370 --> 02:18:51,549
好的。
Okay.

2024
02:18:52,814 --> 02:18:57,870
而且我认为我们花了太多惊人的时间去不去讨论 AI 目前造成的实际危害。
And I think we've spent an alarming amount of time not talking about the actual harms of AI as it is today.

2025
02:18:57,998 --> 02:19:00,770
我认为这些是必须进行的对话。
I think these are necessary conversations to have.

2026
02:19:01,090 --> 02:19:05,870
我认为我们应该谈谈 Amazon、Microsoft、Google 和 Oracle 都在支持这些黑客行为这一事实。
I think we should talk about the fact that Amazon, Microsoft, Google, Oracle are helping power these hacks.

2027
02:19:06,230 --> 02:19:10,770
还有 Sam Altman 和 Dario Amadei 所领导的公司，已经做了等同于重罪级别的黑客攻击。
That Sam Altman and Dario Amadei have overseen companies that have done what is tantamount to felony hacking.

2028
02:19:11,049 --> 02:19:15,370
我们现在讨论的不是如何阻止现在的这些行为，而是明天可能如何阻止。
That we are not having discussions about how to stop this today but what we might stop tomorrow.

2029
02:19:15,709 --> 02:19:19,709
而且我认为总的来说，我们也需要担心那些完全没被提及的财务问题。
And I think in general we also need to worry about the financials which have not come up at all.

2030
02:19:19,950 --> 02:19:25,150
如果行业放缓，该如何处理那 1.3 万亿美元的算力承诺？
But if there is an industry slowdown, how do you deal with the $1.3 trillion of compute commitments?

2031
02:19:25,510 --> 02:19:29,290
所有这些都是非常真实的事情，而且很快就会产生非常现实的后果。
All of these are very real things that will have very real consequences very, very soon.

2032
02:19:29,850 --> 02:19:33,690
但是，我理解原因，而且讨论我们如何应对 AI 是必要的。
But, and I understand why, and it's necessary to discuss what we do around AI.

2033
02:19:34,093 --> 02:19:39,510
我们今天真正需要做的监管措施是切断算力，全面放慢这些实验室的进度。
The actual regulatory thing we need to do today is cut off the compute, slow down these labs fully.

2034
02:19:40,049 --> 02:19:43,129
我在这里并不在乎中国。
And I don't care about China here.

2035
02:19:43,342 --> 02:19:44,170
他们能做什么呢？
What are they gonna do?

2036
02:19:44,290 --> 02:19:46,230
像他们一直以来做的那样去蒸馏模型？
Distill a model like they have the whole time?

2037
02:19:46,450 --> 02:19:47,790
他们在限制我们的进展。
They are capped on our progress.

2038
02:19:47,790 --> 02:19:50,390
所以，最重要的事情就是放慢速度。
So, the biggest thing to do is to slow down.

2039
02:19:50,470 --> 02:19:53,510
而且，是时候开始逮捕相关人员了。
And also, it's time to start arresting people.

2040
02:19:53,890 --> 02:19:55,370
他们犯下了严重的黑客犯罪。
They did felony hacking.

2041
02:19:55,694 --> 02:19:56,790
必须有人入狱。
Someone's got to go to prison.

2042
02:19:57,130 --> 02:19:59,690
我们需要这些公司承担起责任和问责。
We need responsibility and accountability for these companies.

2043
02:19:59,890 --> 02:20:05,270
只要我们不落实问责，那我们就根本没在讨论安全问题，因为我们什么都没做。
And as long as we don't have it, we may as well not have had any discussion about safety, because we're not doing anything.

2044
02:20:05,670 --> 02:20:07,350
Tom Clougherty，你是否承认存在生存层面的风险？
Tom Clougherty Do you accept that there's an existential risk?

2045
02:20:07,662 --> 02:20:08,810
Richard Davis 是的，绝对有。
Richard Davis Yeah, absolutely.

2046
02:20:08,970 --> 02:20:17,850
全球最大的公司正在进行我认为我们都能达成共识的、极其鲁莽的实验，
这些实验动用了数千亿美元的基础设施，而且他们还在全球
We have the largest companies in the world doing what I think we can all agree are extremely reckless experiments using hundreds of
billions of dollars of infrastructure, and they are building more infrastructure

2047
02:20:17,850 --> 02:20:18,800
范围内缓慢增加更多基础设施，来进行更多这种混乱的实验。
We have the largest companies in the world doing what I think we can all agree are extremely reckless experiments using hundreds of
billions of dollars of infrastructure, and they are building more infrastructure

2048
02:20:18,800 --> 02:20:21,990
范围内缓慢增加更多基础设施，来进行更多这种混乱的实验。
around the world very slowly to do more of these chaotic experiments.

2049
02:20:22,310 --> 02:20:23,250
我们必须制约他们。
We must rein them in.

2050
02:20:23,430 --> 02:20:28,710
这并不意味着大语言模型有意识，或者能够做到那些人们承诺过的事情。
This does not mean that large language models are conscious or able to do things that people have been promising.

2051
02:20:29,050 --> 02:20:31,910
确实，它们可能、我不认为它们会，导致你所说的后果。
Indeed, they may, I don't think they will, lead to what you're talking about.

2052
02:20:32,170 --> 02:20:40,610
这并不意味着不存在真实的伤害，但这些是由于某些特定方
在新自由主义的肆虐下被允许肆意妄为而造成的真实伤害。
That doesn't mean there aren't real harms, but these are real harms caused by
very specific parties allowed to run rampant in the scourge of neoliberalism.

2053
02:20:40,610 --> 02:20:41,610
你的概率是多少？
What's your percentage?

2054
02:20:42,830 --> 02:20:44,390
我是说，我们现在讨论的是什么情况？
I mean, what are we talking about here?

2055
02:20:44,430 --> 02:20:47,492
你认为存在超过 10% 的生存层面的伤害风险吗？
Do you think there's a more than 10% chance of existential harm?

2056
02:20:47,670 --> 02:20:49,310
不是在 10 年以内左右吗？
Wasn't it within 10 years or something?

2057
02:20:50,350 --> 02:20:51,110
不是 10%。
Not 10%.

2058
02:20:51,110 --> 02:20:55,310
我是说，你看，哪怕是 1%，但让我把这意味着什么说明白点。
I mean, look, 1%, but it's like, but here's, let me, let me just be clear about what that means.

2059
02:20:55,630 --> 02:21:02,850
我是否认为与大量基础设施相关的、不受限制的 LLM 使用会导致电力系统崩溃？
Do I think that unrestrained LLM use connected to massive amounts of infrastructure could lead to actually a power system going down?

2060
02:21:03,310 --> 02:21:03,710
绝对会。
Absolutely.

2061
02:21:04,030 --> 02:21:05,110
我们有 knight capital 的案例。
We had night capital.

2062
02:21:05,450 --> 02:21:09,930
嗯，大概在 13、14 年前，我能看到有人愚蠢到把那个连接到金融账户上。
Well, like 13, 14 years ago, I could see someone being dumb enough to connect that to financial accounts.

2063
02:21:09,930 --> 02:21:14,790
这种人为错误，结合我们使用的这些混乱的软件，是一个巨大的隐患。
This human error, led with this chaotic software we use, is a danger.

2064
02:21:15,650 --> 02:21:22,430
在方向上我同意逮捕相关人员，但如果你在那些实验室工作，就别去构建通用超智能了。
I will directionally agree with arresting everyone, but don't build general superintelligence if you're working at one of those labs.

2065
02:21:22,670 --> 02:21:22,990
今天就辞职。
Quit today.

2066
02:21:23,370 --> 02:21:23,550
没错。
Hell yeah.

2067
02:21:23,690 --> 02:21:23,810
谢谢。
Thank you.

2068
02:21:24,398 --> 02:21:28,530
这些实验室的人确实认为这构成了灭绝威胁。
The people at these labs really do believe this poses an extinction threat.

2069
02:21:31,054 --> 02:21:38,980
我认为我们作为社会的反应，不能是「请继续，我们希望你们失败」。
I think our response as a society cannot be, please continue, we hope you'll fail.

2070
02:21:39,470 --> 02:21:48,100
我们的社会反应也不能是让你们在你们自己都说不想参与的巨大竞争竞赛中肆意妄为。
And our response as a society cannot be let it rip in a giant competitive race that you yourselves are saying you don't want to be in.

2071
02:21:49,966 --> 02:21:53,060
因为所谓的中国威胁，我们正强迫你们继续前进。
We are forcing you to go ahead because of the boogeyman of China.

2072
02:21:54,030 --> 02:22:05,500
我们看到这些公司的员工表示，我们需要开发工具来控制前沿技术的节奏，这是企业术语，
意思就是这玩意儿发展得太快，我们搞不定了。
We have seen the people at these companies say that we need to develop the tools to pace the frontier,
which is corporate speak for this is going too fast for us to get a handle on things.

2073
02:22:08,078 --> 02:22:11,890
我们需要……这些人是这么认为的。
We need, like, these people believe it.

2074
02:22:12,846 --> 02:22:14,310
他们认为他们在拿你们的生命做赌注。
They believe they're gambling with your lives.

2075
02:22:14,470 --> 02:22:19,970
变化在于，世界上的其他地方开始注意到这一点，这给了我们一丝希望。
What has changed is that the rest of the world is starting to notice, and that's what gives us a moment of hope.

2076
02:22:20,750 --> 02:22:24,350
本周 Trump 被问到关于 AI 的威胁，这就是他的回答。
Trump this week was asked about the threat of AI, and this was his response.

2077
02:22:24,622 --> 02:22:34,276
关于 AI 的最坏情况是机器人、机器学会了……显然它会自己思考，这就是它的功能，
而这可能会反戈一击，对人类产生威胁。
Case scenario with AI is that the robots, the machinery learns to, obviously it
thinks for itself, that's what it does, and that could turn against humanity.

2078
02:22:34,670 --> 02:22:36,390
我们有护栏吗？
Do we have the guardrails?

2079
02:22:36,622 --> 02:22:37,270
没事的。
It's going to be fine.

2080
02:22:37,330 --> 02:22:39,050
我们总会有办法阻止它们的，对吧？
We'll always have something to stop them, right?

2081
02:22:39,390 --> 02:22:40,110
我们会有一点手段。
We'll have a little gear.

2082
02:22:40,770 --> 02:22:41,430
我真心希望如此。
I really hope so.

2083
02:22:41,970 --> 02:22:43,450
我真的很不喜欢那个机器人。
I really don't like that robot.

2084
02:22:43,630 --> 02:22:44,068
我们会阻止它的。
We'll stop it.

2085
02:22:44,690 --> 02:22:46,210
有些人说这是最坏的情况。
Some people say the worst case scenario.

2086
02:22:46,490 --> 02:22:49,390
你在笑，但这就是目前 AI 安全领域的最前沿技术。
You're laughing, but this is the state of the art in AI safety right now.

2087
02:22:49,470 --> 02:22:49,650
是的。
Yeah.

2088
02:22:49,950 --> 02:22:51,310
这就是我们现有的手段。
This is the device we have.

2089
02:22:51,410 --> 02:22:52,230
这就是我们最好的办法了。
That's the best we got.

2090
02:22:52,330 --> 02:22:56,010
对于没听清的人，Trump 说：我们一直都会没事的，我们总有办法控制它。
For anyone that couldn't hear that, Trump went, we'll always be fine, we'll have something to control it.

2091
02:22:56,050 --> 02:22:58,730
然后他做了一个枪指的手势，并说：砰，我不喜欢那个机器人。
And then he did a little gun finger and he went, boom, I don't like that robot.

2092
02:22:59,850 --> 02:23:00,930
我不喜欢 Sammy。
I don't like Sammy.

2093
02:23:02,070 --> 02:23:03,050
如果你不笑话的话。
If you don't laugh.

2094
02:23:03,726 --> 02:23:16,810
嗯，我想说，人类之所以总能解决问题，是因为人们发现了问
题并制造出必要的手段来阻止它，我觉得你也会同意我的观点。
Uh, I would say that the reason humanity always has something to stop a problem is because people notice a problem and build what it
takes to have something to stop a problem, which I think you'd agree with.

2095
02:23:17,358 --> 02:23:19,090
我不是站在这里说我们要死。
I am not here saying we're going to die.

2096
02:23:19,630 --> 02:23:34,250
我是想说，如果你看这项技术，看看它现在在做什么，再看看那些构建它的专家对他们自身担忧
的表态，你会发现我们需要挺过这个关口。
I'm here saying if you look at the technology, if you look at what it's doing now,
if you look at what the experts who are building it are saying about their own fears, you see that we need to rise to this occasion.

2097
02:23:35,118 --> 02:23:36,710
你刚才说你相信人类能够挺过这个关口。
You said you trust humanity to rise to the occasion.

2098
02:23:37,358 --> 02:23:38,530
我真心希望我们能做到。
I sure hope we can.

2099
02:23:38,926 --> 02:23:47,630
我认为应对这一挑战意味着没人会去盲目追求超智能，因为我们根本不知道该如何正确处理它。
I think that rising to this occasion is gonna mean that nobody races
towards superintelligence because we have no idea how to get that right.

2100
02:23:48,330 --> 02:23:54,330
而且，你知道的，世界终于开始意识到这是一种灭绝威胁。
And, you know, finally the world is starting to notice that it's an extinction threat.

2101
02:23:55,390 --> 02:23:55,650
谢谢。
Thank you.

2102
02:23:56,078 --> 02:23:59,030
Nate、Roman、Ed、Andy，非常感谢你们。
Nate, Roman, Ed, Andy, super appreciate you.

2103
